this summer pasar was hit by the worst  flooding ever  at the beginning of june 2013 the  historic town  was underwater estimates put the damages  at around 200 million euros  the ashenberger hotel's rosen cafe sits  right on the bank of the danube  refurbishing it as one of 20 projects  this construction contractor is  currently working on  the restaurant owner welcomes him and  shows him what the water damage did  the banquet hall restaurant and outdoor  beer garden are ruined  the owner puts the damage at about 1.3  million euros  he shows the builder a watermark it was  two and a half meters deep in some  places  the sludge and debris have been cleared  but even things that appear unscathed  need to be replaced  has to be removed because the insulation  underneath has to go too  insulation is what absorbs the water and  bacteria and other pathogens could still  be in it so that you can't really get  rid of them  everything has to come out  his workers are taking off the plaster  all the bookings for this summer hotel  reservations and weddings  had to be cancelled  we're at a standstill and we've had no  income for three months not at all  and we still don't know if we can hire  anyone and if so for how much  that's the big problem  the construction contractor has plenty  of customers in the center of paso as  well  he checks in with one of his customers  they agree  things are progressing but slowly  imagine the window is almost covered  plastering work is only just starting  now more than two months after the water  subsided  yet for shop owners every day counts  the merchandise was your capital and  it's been destroyed  you don't even have the financial means  to repair the building  so you have to wait for help  the contractor's order books are filled  to brimming and he already had plenty to  do before the flood  his 150 employees are now doing extra  shifts  he's not even trying to find more  workers everyone is already on the job  at the same time he still has to  continue with major projects  like an extension on the children's  clinic in pasa  but he doesn't want to leave anyone in  the lurch  or because workers are still doing flood  repairs  of course there are others too thank  goodness there aren't that many of them  who say  no we set a deadline and you have to  stick to it period  things in the old town are moving  forward bit by bit  the danube rose to nearly 13 meters it's  an historic benchmark that's been  immortalized on the town hall wall  residents won't soon forget this flood  even if things in pasar appear to be  getting back to normal
 

